=== <span class="anchor" id="Tamaraw FX"></span><span class="anchor" id="Philippines 2"></span> Tamaraw FX (Philippines) ===
[[File:Toyota Tamaraw FX.jpg|thumb|Toyota Tamaraw FX GL (Philippines)]]

In the Philippines, versions of the Kijang were sold as the Tamaraw FX when it was introduced in 1991 as a high side pickup and in 1993 as a wagon.<ref>{{Cite web|date=2015-07-06|title=Toyota First Automaker in the Philippines to Reach One Million Sales Milestone|url=https://global.toyota/en/detail/8638785|website=Toyota Global}}</ref> The Tamaraw FX was available either with a 1.5 5K petrol engine, a 1.8-litre 7K petrol engine or a 2.0-litre ''[[Toyota C engine#2C|2C]]'' [[diesel engine]], all coupled with a 5-speed manual transmission. Power was {{convert|59|kW|hp PS|abbr=on|0}}<ref name="toyota.com.ph">{{Cite web|date=1998-07-01|title=Toyota Motor Philippines: Toyota Tamaraw FX|url=http://www.toyota.com.ph:80/autos/tamfx/performance/7k.html|access-date=2020-07-01|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/19980701231430/http://www.toyota.com.ph:80/autos/tamfx/performance/7k.html|archive-date=1998-07-01}}</ref> and {{convert|51|kW|hp PS|abbr=on|0}}<ref name="toyota.com.ph"/> respectively, allowing for top speeds of {{convert|130|or|125|km/h|mph|0|abbr=on}}. The Tamaraw FX was offered in 5 door long wheelbase wagon in either Standard,<ref>{{Cite web|date=1998-07-01|title=Toyota Motor Philippines: Toyota Tamaraw FX|url=http://www.toyota.com.ph:80/autos/tamfx/variation/standard.html|access-date=2020-07-01|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/19980701231717/http://www.toyota.com.ph:80/autos/tamfx/variation/standard.html|archive-date=1998-07-01}}</ref> Deluxe<ref>{{Cite web|date=1998-07-01|title=Toyota Motor Philippines: Toyota Tamaraw FX|url=http://www.toyota.com.ph:80/autos/tamfx/variation/deluxe.html|access-date=2020-07-01|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/19980701231711/http://www.toyota.com.ph:80/autos/tamfx/variation/deluxe.html|archive-date=1998-07-01}}</ref> and GL trim levels.<ref>{{Cite web|date=1998-07-01|title=Toyota Motor Philippines: Toyota Tamaraw FX|url=http://www.toyota.com.ph:80/autos/tamfx/variation/gl.html|access-date=2020-07-01|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/19980701231704/http://www.toyota.com.ph:80/autos/tamfx/variation/gl.html|archive-date=1998-07-01}}</ref> The GL trim included power steering, cloth interior, cloth seats, a digital clock, door pocket, a 4 speaker cassette radio, body cladding and 14 inch alloy wheels. Deluxe and standard models had steel wheels, 2 speaker cassette decks, and vinyl interiors, however deluxe models received full width side steps and bucket front seats. Dual AC was standard on all wagon models. The pickup variant was available in 11-seater high side pickup form<ref>{{Cite web|date=1998-07-01|title=Toyota Motor Philippines: Toyota Tamaraw FX|url=http://www.toyota.com.ph/autos/tamfx/variation/hspu.html|access-date=2020-07-01|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/19980701231724/http://www.toyota.com.ph/autos/tamfx/variation/hspu.html|archive-date=1998-07-01}}</ref> and later in chassis cab configuration. In 2000, Deluxe, GL, and diesel wagon variants were discontinued leaving only the 1.8 standard wagon and pickup variants<ref>{{Cite web|title=Tamaraw FX - Main Specs|url=http://www.toyota.com.ph/autos/tamfx/main_specs.html|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20011114030058fw_/http://www.toyota.com.ph/autos/tamfx/main_specs.html|archive-date=14 November 2001|access-date=2020-07-02|website=Toyota Motor Philippines}}</ref> to be sold alongside the Revo until 2002 when the Tamaraw was finally discontinued.

The Tamaraw FX was meant for use as a passenger wagon. It still retained the ruggedness of the old Tamaraw and is ideal for rough roads and carrying heavy loads. It was assembled by [[Toyota Motor Philippines]] in its Parañaque plant that was formerly operated by Delta Motors with the pickup version rolled-out off the assembly line in October 1991 and the wagon version in December 1993.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Socco|first=Vince|date=2018-08-01|title=30 years ago, Toyota commenced its Philippine journey|url=https://visor.ph/industry/30-years-ago-toyota-commenced-its-philippine-journey/|website=Visor}}</ref> Also around that time, after Ford left the Philippines in 1984, Pilipinas Nissan bought the rights to produce the Fiera IV under the name Nissan Bida;<ref name="auv"/> and local car assembler Carter Motors came up with their own version, the Masa ([[Tagalog language|Tagalog]] for "masses", implying that the car is within reach of the masses).{{Citation needed|date=March 2025}} The Tamaraw FW was also widely used by metered taxis and the point-to-point [[UV Express]] system, leading to the colloquial use of the term "FX" to refer to UV Express vehicles regardless of the actual make or model.<ref>{{Cite news |last=De Castro Jr. |first=Isagani |date=2024-11-02 |title=[OPINION] The good, bad (and sad) about the next gen Toyota Tamaraw |url=https://www.rappler.com/business/opinion-design-cost-next-generation-toyota-tamaraw-fx/ |access-date=2025-03-21 |work=[[Rappler]]}}</ref>

