=== <span class="anchor" id="Condor"></span><span class="anchor" id="Stallion 2"></span><span class="anchor" id="Stallion F60"></span> Condor (F80/F81/F85 - African Markets)/Stallion (F60 - South Africa only; Panel Van) ===
The Zace Surf, Condor and Stallion are high-roof station wagon based on the long-wheelbase Kijang wagon. For the right hand drive South African market Condor and was initially offered with a choice of the 1.8-litre ''[[Toyota Y engine#2Y|2Y]]'' engine in the base Estate and Estate TE (later replaced by the 2.0-litre ''1RZ-E'' in the second refresh), 2.4-litre ''[[Toyota RZ engine#2RZ-E|2RZ-E]]'' petrol engine and a 3.0-litre ''[[Toyota L engine#5L|5L]]'' diesel engine in either Estate TE and 4x4 RV trims.<ref name=":11">{{Cite web|date=June 2000|title=Toyota Condor Brochure Part No:MT1311|url=http://www.toyota.co.za:80/ask/pdf/condor.exe|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20020112144219/http://www.toyota.co.za:80/ask/pdf/condor.exe|archive-date=2002-01-12|access-date=2020-10-12|website=Toyota South Africa|type=PDF file in Self Extracting Executable}}</ref> Unique to the Condor is the 7-seat configuration with second row individual seating,<ref name=":11" /><ref>{{Cite web|title=Condor Press Vehicle Reports|url=http://www.toyota.co.za/products/condor_press.asp?vehicle=Condor|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20020627101731/http://www.toyota.co.za:80/products/condor_press.asp?vehicle=Condor|archive-date=2002-06-27|access-date=2020-10-12}}</ref> locks integrated to the inner door handles, Corolla-style outer door handles, and a lack of a rear cooler option. Available options are air conditioning for the base estate, leather seats for the RV (and later TX) or PVC seats (all trims). An 8-seat configuration later replaced the 7-seater with the second row seats being exchanged for a split 60/40 unit in the first refresh with the 4x4 RV trim being replaced by the new TX and TX 4x4 trims.<ref name=":12">{{Cite web|date=June 2002|title=Toyota Condor Brochure Part No:MT1381|url=http://www.toyota.co.za:80/ask/pdf/190874_Condor.exe|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20030814190452/http://www.toyota.co.za:80/ask/pdf/190874_Condor.exe|archive-date=2003-08-14|access-date=2020-10-12|website=Toyota South Africa|type=PDF File in Self Extracting executable}}</ref> The third row bench would get replaced by a 50/50 side folding unit in the second and final refresh model which also included the front end design as well as the rear taillights from the second facelift Kijang while still retaining cloth interior from the first Kijang facelift and the use of rubber seals in the rear window.<ref name=":13">{{Cite web|date=September 2003|title=Toyota Condor Brochure Part No:MT1408|url=http://www.toyota.co.za/ask/pdf/190874_Condor.exe|url-status=bot: unknown|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201018011946/https://www.toyota.co.za/ask/pdf/190874_Condor.exe|archive-date=2020-10-18 |access-date=2020-10-12|website=Toyota South Africa|type=PDF file in Self Extracting Executable}}</ref> The 2.4-litre petrol engine was also now made available to the base Estate trim alongside the new 2.0-litre engine while a driver airbag was added to the TX trim. The Condor was sold as well in Left hand drive Ghana and Nigerian markets with 1.8-litre ''2Y'' and 3.0-litre ''5L'' engines with a 10-seat configuration with a 4x4 option being available to the diesel models.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.toyotaghana.com/html/cars/condor.html|title=Toyota Ghana Limited: Condor |date=2005-02-06|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20050206065944/http://www.toyotaghana.com/html/cars/condor.html|archive-date=2005-02-06|url-status=bot: unknown|access-date=2017-10-17}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2005-11-15 |title=Toyota Nigeria Limited - Condor Model Variations |url=http://www.toyotanigeria.com/products/condor_model.html |access-date=2022-02-23 |website= |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20051115061129/http://www.toyotanigeria.com/products/condor_model.html |archive-date=15 November 2005 |url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2002-06-20 |title=Toyota Condor - Model |url=http://toyotanigeria.com/condor/modelcondor.htm |access-date=2022-02-23 |website= |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20020620151930/http://toyotanigeria.com/condor/modelcondor.htm |archive-date=20 June 2002 |url-status=dead}}</ref>

The Stallion Panel Van was introduced initially with a 1.8-litre ''2Y'' engine sharing the F60 designation with the Kijang/Zace pickup<ref>{{Cite web|date=June 2000|title=Toyota Condor Brochure Part No:MT1319|url=http://www.toyota.co.za:80/ask/pdf/stallion.exe|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20030814190959/http://www.toyota.co.za:80/ask/pdf/stallion.exe|archive-date=2003-08-14|access-date=2020-10-12|website=Toyota South Africa|type=PDF file in Self Extracting Executable}}</ref> (later replaced by a 2.0-litre ''1RZ-E'' unit as a base engine in 2003 for both the South African market Condor and Stallion models)<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://carmag.co.za/news/buying-used-cars/buying-used-toyota-condor-2000-2005/|title=Buying used: Toyota Condor (2000-2005)|last=Palm|first=Peter|website=CAR magazine|date=21 October 2011|language=en|access-date=2020-02-10}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|date=September 2003|title=Toyota Condor Brochure Part No:MT1403|url=http://www.toyota.co.za/ask/pdf/stallion.exe|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20040321212156/http://www.toyota.co.za/ask/pdf/stallion.exe|archive-date=2004-03-21|access-date=2020-10-12|website=Toyota South Africa|type=PDF file in Self Extracting Executable}}</ref> in a 3-seat configuration with no second row seats. Immobilisers were standard equipment throughout the entire South African Condor/Stallion (non 2Y SA models use a transponder key immobiliser) range while included as part of the 4x4 model in Ghana markets.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.toyotaghana.com/html/cars/toyotabrochure.pdf|title=Toyota Brochure|date=2004-12-05|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20041205115251/http://www.toyotaghana.com/html/cars/toyotabrochure.pdf|archive-date=2004-12-05|access-date=2020-02-10}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.wheels24.co.za/NewModels/2003-Toyota-Condor-20030729|title=2003 Toyota Condor|date=2003-07-29|website=Wheels24|language=en|access-date=2020-02-10}}</ref>

==== Racing ====
The Team Castrol Toyota Condor 2.4 RV 4x4 was prepared by Toyota South Africa's Research and Development Team in [[Prospecton|Prospecton, Durban]] for the South Africa National Off-Road Championship.<ref name=":9">{{Cite web|title=Toyota to Focus 2003 Motor Sport Programme on Rallying and Off-road Racing |url=https://carmag.co.za/motorsport-news-blog/toyota-to-focus-2003-motor-sport-programme-on-rallying-and-off-road-racing/|access-date=2020-10-12|website=CARMag|date=25 November 2002 |language=en}}</ref><ref name=":10">{{cite magazine|last=Schoeman|first=Deon|date=February 2002|title=Toyota Condor RV Off-road Racer - Impressions |magazine=Topcar|location=South Africa|publisher=Media24|pages=67–70}}</ref> Introduced in the 2001 season it was driven by Kassie Coetzee and co-driver Ockie Fourie until the 2003 season where it was replaced by a Hilux 3.0TD 4x4.<ref name=":9" /> Competing in the 4-cylinder production class category (Class E) modifications include an uprated engine (initially to {{convert|155|PS|kW hp|0|abbr=on}}, later increased to {{convert|170|PS|kW hp|0|abbr=on}}), Addition of a rear limited slip differential, a 260-litre fuel tank, strengthened suspension, as well a [[roll cage]] and associated safety equipment.<ref name=":10" /> A heavy-duty clutch and high-temperature brake compound were fitted to aid endurance and a limiting valve was fitted to adjust brake bias however the rest of the brakes, gearbox, and [[transfer case]] remains standard.<ref name=":10" /> The vehicle was competitive even in its debut season, but found limited success throughout its racing career with only a single class win (7th overall) from the opening race of the 2002 season (Barberspan 500) before suffering multiple mishaps in the 2002 season that included a rock-throwing incident.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Clean sweep for Toyota |url=https://carmag.co.za/motorsport-news-blog/clean-sweep-for-toyota/ |access-date=2020-10-12 |website=CARMag |date=30 April 2002 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Toyota Aiming for Another 1-2-3 in Sugarbelt 400 |url=https://carmag.co.za/motorsport-news-blog/toyota-aiming-for-another-1-2-3-in-sugarbelt-400/ |access-date=2020-10-12 |website=CARMag |date=8 May 2002 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Toyota Keeps Up the Pressure in Chase for Off-road Championships |url=https://carmag.co.za/motorsport-news-blog/toyota-keeps-up-the-pressure-in-chase-for-off-road-championships/ |access-date=2020-10-12 |website=CARMag |date=4 October 2002 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Good result for Toyota's Class T Supertruck Land Cruiser |url=https://carmag.co.za/motorsport-news-blog/good-result-for-toyotas-class-t-supertruck-land-cruiser/ |access-date=2020-10-12 |website=CARMag |date=28 June 2002 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Team Castrol Toyota Gearing Up for Toyota Dealer 400|url=https://carmag.co.za/motorsport-news-blog/team-castrol-toyota-gearing-up-for-toyota-dealer-400/|access-date=2020-10-12|website=CARMag|date=27 August 2002 |language=en}}</ref>

=== <span class="anchor" id="Zace Surf"></span>Zace (F80 - Vietnam/F60 - Taiwan only; Pickup)/Zace Surf (F80/F84/F85 - Taiwan) ===

==== Taiwan ====
The left-hand drive Zace Surf for the Taiwanese market was available in both 5 and 8-seater configurations with a choice of either a 1.8-litre or 2.4-litre petrol engine. Anti-lock brakes were available with the 1.8-litre petrol engine as standard in Urban Wagon and pre-facelift GL models while available as an option in GL Limited, DX-Limited and DX trims with all 2.4-litre models receiving anti lock brakes as standard.<ref name=":2">{{Cite web|url=http://www.toyota.com.tw/upload/CTContent/%7BD5DBBCD2-5CC1-432E-8444-69F438A3A162%7D/SURF.pdf|title=Zace Surf Brochure|date=2006-06-27|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060627193129/http://www.toyota.com.tw/upload/CTContent/%7BD5DBBCD2-5CC1-432E-8444-69F438A3A162%7D/SURF.pdf|archive-date=2006-06-27|access-date=2020-02-10}}</ref><ref name=":3">{{Cite web|url=https://www.7car.tw/articles/read/16113|title=Toyota Zace Surf 原廠型錄|website=7car.tw 小七車觀點|access-date=2020-02-10}}</ref> A driver airbag was also standard equipment for Urban Wagon and pre-facelift GL models as well as in 4WD trims.<ref name=":3" /><ref name=":2" /> The 8-seat configuration using a 50/50 split side folding unit with third row seatbelts as used in the Unser and late model Condors was initially offered as a trim level to both 1.8-litre<ref name=":3" /> and 2.4-litre 4WD<ref name=":6">{{Cite web|title=Toyota VX4 8人自排(4WD)詳細規格/配備表|url=http://www.toyota.com.tw:80/DOWNLOAD/CAR/VX2.PDF|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20020608133931/http://www.toyota.com.tw:80/DOWNLOAD/CAR/VX2.PDF|archive-date=June 8, 2002|access-date=2020-07-22|website=Toyota Taiwan}}</ref> models with the latter being discontinued in 2002.<ref name=":6" /><ref>{{Cite web|title=Toyota Surf 詳細規格／配備表|url=http://www.toyota.com.tw:80/CAR2002/DOC/SURF.PDF|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20021007170058/http://www.toyota.com.tw:80/CAR2002/DOC/SURF.PDF|archive-date=2002-10-07|access-date=2020-07-22|website=Toyota Taiwan}}</ref> Both models are uncommon as owners opted to install aftermarket bench seats to 5-seater models instead. Refreshed models more closely mimic the changes made in the Kijang's second refresh that included the suede interior as well the use of compound sealant for the rear glass. Unique to the Zace Surf is the use of a printed window antenna, the option of a roof mounted VCD player and a car phone in later, higher end trims like the VX-surf or VX Prerunner.<ref name=":2" /> All Zace Surfs with the 2.4-litre engine use the 4x4's taller suspension unlike the Condor. 4x4 models uses a full-time 4WD system with a lockable centre differential and high/low range transfer case.<ref name=":11" /><ref name=":13" /><ref name=":12" />

==== Vietnam ====
The Toyota Zace was introduced to Vietnam in 1999 as the GL and DX equipped with 1.8 litre EFI petrol engine with {{cvt|83|hp|kW|0|order=flip}} at 4,800 rpm. The DX version has a lower comfort profile than the GL version with a manual external rearview mirror, no front fog lights and no tachometer. In 2000, the Zace got a redesigned front and the 1.8-litre engine was increased to 2.0-litres. The second upgrade was introduced in 2003 with an improved front and rear.

In 2004, Toyota introduced the GL limited with 200 units produced, a revised exterior, wood-panelled interior, and upholstered seats.

In 2005, Toyota introduced the Zace Surf with an improved exterior that included larger wings and front bumper, chrome exterior door handles and windshield surrounds, and 15-inch wheels. The interior was equipped with a wood-panelled console, CD player, 6-speaker audio and leather seats.

The Zace was a successful model and also the best-selling model of Toyota Motor Vietnam in 2005 with 5,634 units. The total number of cars sold from launch to the end of production was 17,268 units.{{Citation needed|date=October 2022}}

